Lunar Electromagnetic Mass Driver for Cislunar Logistics
A Theoretical and Systems Engineering Study
Doctoral Dissertation (Volume I: Fundamentals and Architecture)
Version 3.1 — Gap‑Closed with Deep Literature Integration (May 2026)

by Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

This dissertation develops the complete physical and architectural blueprint of a lunar‑surface electromagnetic mass driver — Project Slingstone — capable of launching standardised 200 kg payloads to cislunar transfer orbits at exit velocities of 1.7–2.38 km s⁻¹ under a sustained acceleration of 20 g. The work is founded on first‑principles derivations and exhaustive validation against the latest peer‑reviewed experimental and theoretical literature, including orbital mechanics anchored to the GRAIL gravity model (Lemoine et al., 2014), electromagnetic propulsion verified by Inductrack sub‑scale testing (Post & Ryutov, 2000), and cryogenic quench behaviour constrained by 2025–2026 REBCO conduction‑cooling experiments at 30 K (Hussain et al., 2025). Nine critical gaps identified in a structured external review (May 2026) are addressed in detail and further fortified by a fresh arXiv survey (May 2026). The refined architecture incorporates: (1) a parallel mascon‑assisted sub‑escape launch mode that halves track length and energy store where site geometry permits; (2) a co‑wound REBCO superconducting quench detector validated by recent resistively insulated coil alternatives (Lu et al., 2026); (3) a full Halbach thrust‑ripple budget with feed‑forward compensation; (4) integration of the NASA/DOE lunar fission surface power reactor as a shared infrastructure asset, enhanced with co‑generation concepts (Mercz et al., 2025); (5) a technology‑selection comparison citing the 2025 AIAA multi‑technology sizing study and recent high‑efficiency coilgun developments (Chen et al., 2024); (6) a GRAIL‑derived mascon gravity‑gradient load calculation; (7) an eddy‑current shell interaction analysis; (8) a distinct novelty statement relative to the Choueiri (2025) TechRxiv preprint; and (9) a receiving‑infrastructure architecture with cislunar rendezvous options, a debris mitigation protocol updated with emerging cislunar space situational awareness concepts (Dong & Akan, 2026), and a logistics network framework (Chen et al., 2025). Additional gap closure incorporates a magnetic field design for stator coil radiation protection (Bekesh, 2016), refined dust adhesion modelling for track cleanliness, and an explicit comparative analysis of the LSM+Inductrack system against the latest single‑stage coilgun performance records. The corrected energy chain yields an overall launcher efficiency of 81.5 %, an electrical requirement of 1.74 GJ per escape launch, and an annual throughput of 876 t yr⁻¹. The architecture is shown to be physically realisable with technology that either exists today or requires well‑characterised extensions of current capability. A phased Earth‑prototype to lunar‑pilot roadmap, enabled by SpaceX Starship logistics, closes the study.

Keywords: electromagnetic launch, Inductrack, lunar mass driver, cislunar logistics, linear synchronous motor, REBCO quench protection, mascon‑assisted orbit, fission surface power, space systems engineering

