1.3 Methodology and Novelty Statement

No physical prototypes were built and no machine‑learning model was trained. The analysis follows three parallel tracks: (1) analytical physics from Maxwell’s equations, two‑body orbital mechanics, and Fourier heat conduction; (2) literature‑anchored validation against published experimental data, including Inductrack 1/4‑scale tests, HTS conductor characterisation (MISSE‑13; Hussain et al., 2025), GRAIL gravity models, and the latest commercial power‑semiconductor releases; (3) conservative system‑engineering margins exceeding NASA‑STD‑5001B minima.

This thesis contributes the first: (i) full systems‑engineering architecture with an FMEA tied to experimental benchmarks; (ii) Inductrack‑specific efficiency derivation with segment‑by‑segment loss accounting; (iii) Shackleton‑site‑specific design integrating solar illumination, fission surface power, and dust mitigation; (iv) terminal‑guidance subsystem with quadrupole‑field steering; and (v) formal technology‑selection comparison across coilgun, railgun, and LSM architectures that now incorporates the latest high‑efficiency coilgun results (Chen et al., 2024). All gaps identified in a May 2026 external review and subsequent arXiv survey have been closed, as documented throughout.

