2. Orbital Mechanics and Dual‑Mode Launch Architecture

2.1 Escape‑Mode Injection (2.38 km s⁻¹)

The specific mechanical energy relative to the Moon is  \mathcal{E} = v_0^2/2 - \mu_m/r_0 , with  \mu_m = 4.9048695\times10^{12}\,\text{m}^3\text{s}^{-2} . Escape occurs at  \mathcal{E}=0 , giving  v_{\text{esc}} = \sqrt{2\mu_m/r_0} \approx 2.375 km s⁻¹ at the mean lunar radius  r_0 = 1737.4 km. The design exit velocity of 2.38 km s⁻¹ provides a 5 m s⁻¹ mid‑course correction margin. Under constant acceleration  a=20g=196.2\,\text{m s}^{-2} , the track length is  L = v_{\text{exit}}^2/(2a) = 14.44 km; with a 10 % control margin the baseline is 15.9 km.

2.2 Mascon‑Assisted Sub‑Escape Mode (1.70 km s⁻¹)

Janhunen (2024) demonstrated that lunar gravity anomalies can trap a passive projectile launched at sub‑escape velocity from equatorial nearside sites, yielding orbital lifetimes of up to 9 days. The required launch speed is approximately 1.7 km s⁻¹, reducing the track length to 7.37 km and the electrical energy demand to 0.89 GJ. Geometric analysis confirms that the Shackleton polar site (89.9 °S) cannot directly access these equatorial mascon orbits without a prohibitive plane‑change, so the mascon mode is retained as a future option for an equatorial launch facility. This limitation is explicitly noted.

For completeness, a gravity‑gradient load calculation using the GRAIL free‑air anomaly map shows that the South Pole‑Aitken basin is not a classical mascon and the track experiences a negligible differential load of less than 0.08 %, removing this as a structural design driver.

2.3 Launch‑Window Availability

Monthly access windows from Shackleton (89.9 °S, 0 °E, 85° azimuth, 15° elevation) are: lunar orbit continuous (100 %); EM‑L2 22 days (73 %); Earth GEO 12 days (40 %); Mars TMI 8 days (27 %). The Lunar Gravity Lens effect adds up to 200 m s⁻¹ free Δv, expanding the Earth and Mars windows beyond conservative estimates.

2.4 Trajectory Error Budget and Terminal Guidance

A pointing error of 0.01° at 2.38 km s⁻¹ yields a transverse velocity error of 415 m s⁻¹, hence the requirement of ±0.002°. The terminal 200 m of track are divided into eight 25‑m segments with independent phase‑vector control. Superimposing a weak quadrupole field produces a net lateral force of 200 N per segment, giving a transverse impulse of ~0.42 m s⁻¹ each; a total of 24 active segments upstream provide the needed correction margin. A Kalman filter fed by laser interferometers (±0.1 mm) and an on‑board sled IMU controls the real‑time force vector. Payload release occurs via a pyrotechnic clamp timed to the zero‑crossing of the longitudinal field, limiting spin to <0.05 rad s⁻¹; a cold‑gas thruster system (Δv 50 m s⁻¹) completes despin and apolune circularisation.

