3. Electromagnetic Propulsion Physics

3.1 Linear Synchronous Motor (LSM) and Propulsion Force

The stator is a three‑phase double‑sided LSM with copper Litz‑wire windings cooled to 30 K. The sled carries a Halbach array of Sm₂Co₁₇ permanent magnets (superior radiation hardness; see Section 8). For a gap  g=50 mm, the peak air‑gap flux density is conservatively  B_g = 1.1 T. The resulting magnetic pressure  p_m = B_g^2/(2\mu_0) \approx 0.48 MPa. To accelerate a total mass of 500 kg (200 kg payload + 300 kg sled) at 20 g, the required force is 98.1 kN, demanding a magnet area of 0.204 m², well within the sled base.

3.2 Inductrack Levitation and High‑Speed Drag

The Halbach array induces currents in a stationary ladder track, providing repulsive lift. The lift‑to‑drag ratio is  L/D = \omega L_t/R_t , with  \omega = v/\lambda  and  \lambda=0.25 m. Extrapolating from measured L/D ≥ 200:1 at 139 m s⁻¹ (Post & Ryutov, 2000), a conservative L/D = 100 is adopted at exit speed. The drag power is 19.3 kW, negligible compared with ohmic heating.

3.3 Low‑Speed Transition and Active Stability Control

Below  v_t \approx 8 m s⁻¹ the sled rests on auxiliary ceramic‑ball‑bearing wheels; from 8–15 m s⁻¹ active vertical damping stabilises the transition. A state‑space model confirms that passive lateral stiffness (>10⁵ N m⁻¹) combined with active phase‑sequence biasing maintains the 50 mm clearance within ±5 mm throughout.

3.4 Halbach Thrust‑Ripple Budget and Feed‑Forward Compensation

End effects from the finite Halbach array introduce force ripple of ~1–2 % of peak thrust. To prevent constructive accumulation, the Halbach wavelength is set to  \lambda = 0.247 m (non‑commensurable with the 100 m segment length). A feed‑forward current correction cancels the first three harmonic components using the analytical model of Mohammadi et al. (2024), and the terminal Kalman filter removes any residual velocity error.

3.5 Conducting‑Shell Eddy‑Current Analysis

The conductive sled frame and any metallic enclosure would induce eddy currents that sap efficiency. Mitigation: the sled’s carbon‑fibre frame is electrically segmented every 0.25 m; the first 500 m tunnel enclosure uses non‑conducting PET/Kapton laminate; and the small tungsten‑carbide guide skids contribute <0.1 % additional loss. These measures are informed by recent research on eddy‑current effects in launcher barrels (High Voltage, 2025).

3.6 Efficiency Chain

The overall electrical‑to‑kinetic efficiency is the product: battery discharge (0.95) × SiC inverter (0.995) × stator ohmic (0.92 at 30 K) × segment switching and end effects (0.95) × mechanical losses (0.99) = 0.815. Thus, the electrical energy per escape launch is 1.74 GJ, and per mascon launch 0.89 GJ.

3.7 Technology Selection: LSM+Inductrack vs. Coilgun and Railgun

A 2025 AIAA multi‑technology sizing study (AIAA 2025-4123) provides the closest existing benchmark. To this we add the latest single‑stage coilgun results: Chen et al. (2024) demonstrated a coilgun design attaining significantly higher efficiency and velocity than earlier multi‑stage systems, confirming that coilguns remain a rapidly developing competitor. The comparison is summarised:

Criterion Coilgun (latest) Railgun LSM+Inductrack (selected)
Contact‑free operation Partial No (sliding armature) Yes (full levitation)
Wear / refurbishment Moderate High Very low
Efficiency at scale ~60–75 % (theoretical) ~30–50 % 81.5 % (derived)
TRL for 15 km scale 3–4 4–5 4–5
Dust sensitivity High High Low (50 mm clearance)

While coilguns are closing the efficiency gap, the LSM+Inductrack’s contact‑free levitation, inherent scalability to very long tracks, and lower dust sensitivity make it the superior architecture for a high‑cadence lunar logistics system.

