4. Energy Storage and Power Architecture

4.1 Primary Buffer: All‑Solid‑State Battery Bank

A bank of all‑solid‑state lithium‑ion batteries (ASSBs) forms the primary energy buffer. ASSBs have been qualified on the ISS through a 434‑day test with 562 charge‑discharge cycles and no significant degradation, operating from −40 °C to +120 °C (JAXA, 2025). Twenty‑four racks, each storing 100 MJ (27.8 kWh) and capable of 10C discharge, deliver 576 MW peak power. Total stored energy is 2.4 GJ, providing a 1.38× margin over the required 1.74 GJ.

4.2 Fission Surface Power as Shared Infrastructure

The NASA/DOE MOU of January 2026 and Lockheed Martin’s Phase 1A contracts target a 40 kW(e) fission surface power (FSP) reactor. We treat the FSP as a shared lunar grid asset, with Slingstone as an anchor tenant. The FSP provides 40 kW continuously; during the longest eclipse (43 h) it contributes 6.2 GJ, supplementing the battery. Launch cadence is reduced to 2 per day during eclipses, requiring ~3.5 GJ, easily met. The FSP waste heat is routed into the battery vault, eliminating separate thermal control.

A promising augmentation is the MULE co‑generation reactor concept (Mercz et al., 2025), which supplies both electricity and high‑grade process heat. If deployed, MULE could directly power ISRU regolith processing adjacent to the launcher, increasing system synergy and reducing the net energy demand for Space Brick production. This option is retained for Phase 3.

4.3 Power Conditioning and Switching

In March 2026 Wolfspeed commercialised the first 10 kV SiC MOSFET. With an intrinsic lifetime exceeding 10⁵ years and sub‑100 ns switching, this component (TRL 7) replaces earlier spark‑gap switches, enabling efficient, solid‑state segment drivers.

