5. REBCO Superconducting Magnet and Quench Protection System

5.1 Operating Point and Thermal Environment

Twenty REBCO racetrack coils on the sled operate at 30 K under conduction cooling. This temperature avoids the complexity of liquid helium while reducing stator copper resistivity by a factor of ~6. Hussain et al. (2025) performed the first detailed electro‑magneto‑thermal modelling of conduction‑cooled REBCO coils at precisely 30 K, characterising the quench onset dynamics and providing the data needed to design a protection scheme.

5.2 Co‑Wound REBCO Superconducting Quench Detector (SQD)

Voltage‑tap detection is now known to be too slow for insulated REBCO conductors. We adopt the co‑wound REBCO superconducting quench detector introduced by Zgour et al. (2026). Each sled coil is co‑wound with a deoxygenated REBCO SQD tape (reducing  T_c  from ~92 K to ~60 K) that triggers a fast‑dump circuit within 100 ms, keeping the hotspot temperature below 150 K via a 20 mm² copper stabiliser. The TRL of this specific 30 K conduction‑cooled quench regime is honestly assessed at 5, pending further experimental validation.

To ensure comprehensive coverage of the quench protection landscape, we also note the contemporaneous resistively insulated (RI) REBCO coil approach of Lu et al. (2026), which controls turn‑to‑turn contact resistivity to spread the normal zone. While promising, the RI method requires precise manufacturing control of inter‑turn conductivity that remains to be demonstrated in flight‑like vibration environments. The SQD scheme provides a more direct and potentially more robust detection path for the Slingstone application, and is retained as the baseline.

