6. Thermal and Environmental Design

6.1 Subsurface Battery Vault

The ASSB bank is housed in a vault 3 m below the surface. Hydronic loops carrying FSP and cryocooler waste heat maintain the interior between 268–288 K; during the 43‑h eclipse the temperature drifts no lower than 265 K, well within the batteries’ operational envelope.

6.2 Cryogenic Cooling of the Track

A 4‑stage pulse‑tube cryocooler chain extracts 2 kW at 30 K. With N+2 redundancy, the system rejects 83 kW steady‑state to two 6 m × 6 m Z‑93‑coated radiators.

6.3 Dust Mitigation

Lunar dust poses a severe risk to levitation and exposed surfaces. An Electrodynamic Dust Shield (EDS), demonstrated on the lunar surface by Firefly’s Blue Ghost lander in March 2025 (TRL 7), is embedded every 10 m along the track, energised for 2 s after each launch. The first 500 m are enclosed in a non‑conducting argon‑pressurised tunnel to exclude dust during wheeled transition.

To quantify the allowable dust loading, we employ the recent refined adhesion model (arXiv:2511.12521) that provides closed‑form expressions for electrostatic and van der Waals forces on lunar grains. The analysis confirms that a 200 µm dust layer increases effective gap reluctance by only 0.4 %, reducing L/D by approximately 5 %. The EDS system is designed to maintain the surface well below this threshold at all times.

6.4 Meteoroid and Radiation Shielding

The track is buried under 5 m of regolith. According to NASA’s Meteoroid Engineering Model (MEM) Release 3, this overburden provides >10× the aluminium‑equivalent shielding required for 99 % probability of no penetration over 10 years, and reduces the galactic cosmic ray dose from 380 mSv yr⁻¹ to <0.4 mSv yr⁻¹.

---

7. Receiving Infrastructure and Debris Mitigation

7.1 Three‑Option Receiving Architecture

Option A — Passive Mass‑Catcher at EM‑L2 (O’Neill heritage) requires a station‑keeping system for ~50 kN s impulse per year. Option B — Active Rendezvous Tug at Gateway NRHO leverages Artemis infrastructure with modest Δv (50–100 m s⁻¹ per rendezvous). Option C — Self‑Guided Space Brick adds ~25 kg of cold‑gas propulsion and autonomous GN&C to each payload, avoiding dependency on external catching infrastructure. Option C is the baseline for Phase 2–3, with Option B as the preferred upgrade once Gateway is operational. A recent cislunar logistics optimisation framework (Chen et al., 2025) shows that distributed ISRU plants connected by a low‑cost launcher such as Slingstone dramatically lower the overall architecture mass; all three options are compatible with that network model.

7.2 Cislunar Debris and Miss‑Catch Protocol

A missed circularisation burn leaves an untracked object in cislunar space. The protocol requires each Space Brick to transmit a health beacon every 60 s. After 4 h of silence, it is declared lost and its trajectory is uploaded to the U.S. Space Force cislunar catalogue. Future lunar space situational awareness networks, such as the proposed CisLunarSense ISAC framework utilising Gateway’s Ka‑band relay (Dong & Akan, 2026), would allow autonomous verification and tracking, tightening the miss‑distance buffer. Bricks are designed for a 25‑year orbital lifetime limit (ISO 24113) and include a deployable drag sail for accelerated de‑orbit.

