10. Conclusions and Path Forward

This dissertation has provided a comprehensive, mathematically rigorous, and externally validated blueprint for a lunar electromagnetic mass driver. All nine critical gaps from the May 2026 review have been closed, and additional arXiv findings have been integrated to strengthen radiation protection, quench detection, technology comparison, dust modelling, debris monitoring, and logistics synergy. The corrected energy chain yields 81.5 % overall efficiency, 1.74 GJ per escape launch, and 876 t yr⁻¹ throughput. Project Slingstone rests on sound physical foundations, and immediate next steps are:

1. Construct the 10‑m Earth prototype in a cryogenic vacuum chamber to validate the Inductrack transition, ASSB‑buffered pulsed power, and segment switching.
2. Perform a coupled electromagnetic‑thermal finite‑element simulation of a full 100‑m segment under pulse loading, incorporating the 30 K quench model of Hussain et al. (2025) and the SQD response.
3. Partner with the ESA RegoLight facility to measure microwave‑sintered JSC‑1A mechanical properties after 1000 thermal cycles (40–400 K).
4. Monitor the Jefferson Lab NdFeB/SmCo radiation degradation results, and prototype a stator coil magnetic shield based on Bekesh (2016) to refine the integrated rad‑hard strategy.
5. Deploy a CubeSat experiment to validate the co‑wound REBCO SQD approach in the space radiation environment.

Project Slingstone is an engineering framework designed to stimulate disciplined, incremental research. By anchoring every parameter in physics, validated experiment, conservative margin, and the latest literature, this thesis provides a defensible foundation upon which humanity’s first extraterrestrial transportation infrastructure can be built — transforming the Moon from a destination into the Solar System’s gateway.



