where α_Br = −0.025%/°C = −2.5 × 10⁻⁴ /°C for Sm₂Co₁₇. Note that SmCo has a much smaller temperature coefficient than NdFeB (which has α_Br ≈ −0.12%/°C). At T = −243°C (30 K):

B_r(30K) = 1.05 × [1 + (−2.5 × 10⁻⁴) × (30 − 293)] = 1.05 × [1 + 0.06575] = 1.05 × 1.066 = **1.119 T**

SmCo magnets actually strengthen at cryogenic temperatures — this is a further advantage of SmCo over NdFeB for the Slingstone application.

