### B.2 Inductrack Levitation — Full Analytical Solution

**Derivation of the lift force from Faraday's law:**

Consider a single shorted rectangular rung of width D (= 1.0 m), resistance R_t, inductance L_t, located at position x beneath the Halbach array moving at velocity v.

The flux through the rung from the Halbach fundamental harmonic:

Φ(t) = B₀ · D · δ · sin(2πvt/λ)

where δ is the effective penetration depth (≈ √(L_t/R_t) / (2π/λ) in the high-speed limit). The induced EMF is:

ε(t) = −dΦ/dt = −B₀ · D · δ · (2πv/λ) · cos(2πvt/λ)

The induced current (treating each rung as an RL circuit with source ε):

dI/dt + (R_t/L_t) I = ε/L_t

For sinusoidal excitation ε = ε₀ cos(ωt) with ω = 2πv/λ, the steady-state current amplitude is:

I₀ = ε₀ / √(R²_t + ω²L²_t) = ε₀ / (R_t √(1 + (ωL_t/R_t)²))

The force on this current loop in the field B (by the Lorenz force):

The lift force (y-component) per rung:

dF_lift/dx = I₀ · D · B_x · (ωL_t/R_t) / √(1 + (ωL_t/R_t)²)

Summing over all rungs and invoking the large-ωL/R limit:

**F_lift = (B²₀ D² λ) / (4πR_t) × [ωL_t/R_t / (1 + (ωL_t/R_t)²)]** × N_rungs_active

The drag force (x-component):

