### I.2 Radiation Environment Quantification

The Slingstone site at Shackleton (89.9°S) experiences:

GCR dose rate (unshielded): 380 mSv yr⁻¹ (from Slaba et al., 2020)

Attenuation through 5 m regolith (approximately equivalent to 50 cm aluminium):

Attenuation factor ≈ e^(−μ_m × ρ × x) where for GCR protons at ~1 GeV, μ_m/ρ ≈ 5 × 10⁻² cm² g⁻¹

μ_m × ρ × x = 5 × 10⁻² cm² g⁻¹ × 1.5 g cm⁻³ × 500 cm = **37.5**

Attenuation = e^(−37.5) ≈ **5 × 10⁻¹⁷** → effectively zero dose to buried components ✓

For solar energetic protons (SEP, peak event fluence 10¹⁰ cm⁻²):

At 100 MeV proton energy, range in regolith (density 1.5 g cm⁻³) ≈ 10 cm
5 m = 500 cm >> 10 cm → complete shielding ✓

Worst-case Carrington-class event (10¹¹ cm⁻²): range ≈ 30 cm << 500 cm → still fully shielded ✓

**The buried track components receive effectively zero radiation dose.** The only radiation-exposed components are the sled (Halbach magnets) and the surface-mounted solar array, FSP, and radiators.

For the sled (cycling between loading bay and launch, spending ~13.4 s in flight per launch, plus ~2 h in the loading bay per cycle):

Total annual dose to sled: 380 mSv yr⁻¹ × fraction of time unshielded
Fraction unshielded = 13.4 s launch / 7,200 s cycle = 0.00186 → **0.71 mSv yr⁻¹**

For Sm₂Co₁₇ magnets, the radiation tolerance is > 10⁴ Gy before measurable demagnetisation. At 0.71 mSv yr⁻¹ = 0.71 × 10⁻³ Gy yr⁻¹:

Time to 10⁴ Gy = 10⁴ / (0.71 × 10⁻³) = **1.41 × 10⁷ years** — the magnets are effectively radiation-immune during normal operations.

However, during a large SEP event (Carrington-class), the sled may be exposed to a short-duration high-flux event if it is in the loading bay (above ground). The dose from a single Carrington-class event is estimated at ~10 Gy. Over 30 years, ~10 such events → 100 Gy cumulative — still well below the 10⁴ Gy tolerance. ✓

